## Chapter 4: Solution Design I: Model Selection and Agent Architectures — Flashcards

| # | Front (Question) | Back (Answer) |
|---|-----------------|---------------|
| 1 | Three Claude model tiers — general positioning? | Opus = highest capability/cost/latency (complex reasoning); Sonnet = balanced quality/cost/speed (default production choice); Haiku = fastest/cheapest (high-volume, simple tasks). Exam tip: tier selection is a 3-way tradeoff, not 'pick the best model'. |
| 2 | When should you choose a deterministic workflow over an autonomous agent? | When the sequence of steps is fixed and doesn't change based on intermediate results. Workflows are cheaper, more predictable, and easier to test. Exam tip: 'fixed pipeline' wording = workflow, not agent. |
| 3 | When does an autonomous single agent outperform a deterministic workflow? | When the next step depends on unpredictable intermediate results (tool output, user intent) and the agent must reason about what to do next. Exam tip: look for 'depends on findings' language. |
| 4 | Core justification for a multi-agent/subagent architecture over a single agent? | Task decomposes into independent, parallelizable subtasks each needing isolated context (avoids context pollution). Exam trap: 'task is complex' alone does NOT justify multi-agent. |
| 5 | Primary cost risk of multi-agent orchestration? | Orchestration overhead: each subagent adds its own token cost plus the orchestrator's coordination/aggregation cost. Multi-agent is almost always MORE expensive than single-agent for the same task. Exam trap: 'multi-agent is cheaper' is a wrong-answer distractor. |
| 6 | What API parameter controls extended thinking depth? | A thinking budget (budget_tokens) allocated to the reasoning process. Exam tip: budget should scale with task complexity — maxing it for simple classification wastes cost/latency with no quality gain. |
| 7 | Best practice for routing high-volume, low-complexity requests at enterprise scale? | Tier the model: use the fast/cheap tier for first-pass classification/simple responses, escalate only ambiguous or complex cases to a higher tier. Exam tip: this cascade/tiering pattern is heavily tested. |
| 8 | How should structured output for automated downstream systems be produced? | Use schema-constrained/tool-call based structured output, not free-form text parsed with regex. Guarantees reliably valid output. Exam tip: free-text + regex parsing is the common wrong-answer distractor. |
| 9 | Opus vs Sonnet vs Haiku: which dimensions trade off together? | Latency and cost rise together as capability/quality tier increases (Opus = highest on all three; Haiku = lowest on all three). Exam tip: framed as a 3-way tradeoff triangle — optimizing one usually costs on the others. |
| 10 | Claude Agent SDK pattern for delegating isolated subtasks to separate agent instances? | Subagent / multi-agent orchestration — an orchestrator spawns subagents, each with its own context and tool access, then aggregates results. Exam tip: correct answer for 'parallel independent research' scenarios. |
| 11 | Scenario: fixed ETL-like pipeline (fetch→validate→transform→store), no variability. Best architecture? | Deterministic workflow — don't give an autonomous agent control over a sequence that never changes. Exam tip: don't over-engineer when control flow is fixed. |
| 12 | Why choose Sonnet as the default production tier over Opus or Haiku? | Sonnet typically offers the best balance of quality, latency, and cost for general production workloads, reserving Opus for high-value/complex reasoning and Haiku for high-volume/simple tasks. Exam tip: 'balanced default' scenarios point to Sonnet. |
